WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.036
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented actress

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:04.704
you know from her work
on "Dawson's Creek"

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:07.507
and films like "Batman Begins"
and "All We Had."

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:10.877
She produced, wrote, directed
and stars in "Alone Together"

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:14.847
which is in select theaters this
Friday and On Demand July 29th.

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:15.882
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:21.287
-This is, um,
34 Cherry Lane, right?

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:23.423
-34, yeah.
Yeah, it is.

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:28.394
-My boyfriend booked this place
on Airbnb for a week.

00:00:28.394 --> 00:00:33.500
- hate to break it to you,
but I -- I reserved this Airbnb.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.500
-What?

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.001
-Yeah, my friend
Jimmy hooked me up.

00:00:36.001 --> 00:00:37.636
I-I booked the Airbnb.

00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:38.905
I booked it yesterday.

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:42.475
-Oh, well...

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:45.211
we booked it on Sunday.

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:46.613
-Well, I don't know
what to tell you.

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:48.147
I signed for this yesterday.

00:00:48.147 --> 00:00:51.384
I got an email confirmation
if you want to check it out.

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:54.887
-Please welcome to the show
Katie Holmes, everybody!

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:56.689
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:56.689 --> 00:01:04.597 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:08.634 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:10.670
Welcome to the show.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:11.938
-Thank you.

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:14.173
-You look lovely,
and I really want to stress

00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:16.308
for everybody watching at home
right now that if we were

00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:19.578
wearing our outfits that we have
on, outside in the New York City

00:01:19.578 --> 00:01:21.313
weather right now,
we would be so miserable.

00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:22.482
-Yes.
-Yes.

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:24.217
This is very much
the air conditioned studio.

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:26.218
Don't be watching at home
thinking, New York must be

00:01:26.218 --> 00:01:27.787
nice and cozy right now.

00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:29.321
No, it's real bad.

00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:30.857
-It's real bad right now, yeah.

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:33.359
-This is very exciting.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:35.127
This is the second time
you've directed a film?

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:37.830
-Yes.
-And I'm wondering, did you feel

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:41.467
more confident going
into it a second time?

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:45.037
-Yes, and also,
because it came --

00:01:45.037 --> 00:01:47.941
we shot a year after lockdown.

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:51.611
And the movie takes
place during lockdown.

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:55.281
As actors, I think we all went
through that moment of,

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.682
are we ever going to work again?

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:58.384
Are we ever going
to be on set again?

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:02.055
So it was more about,
oh, my God, there's tape,

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:05.391
there's a mark,
we have lines, wow.

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:06.493
We can do it again.

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:09.996
So it was --
it was actually just --

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:11.831
it never gets easier because

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:13.566
there's always
something to figure out,

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:16.735
but it was definitely, like,
thank you, God,

00:02:16.735 --> 00:02:19.405
for every moment, you know?
-It's nice.

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:23.008
I mean, I think everybody felt
very grateful to be able

00:02:23.008 --> 00:02:25.611
to get back to work,
to some sense of normalcy.

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:28.748
But in order to, you know,
to start filming a film

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:30.850
a year into lockdown, that meant
that you were writing it

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.352
sort of right
at the beginning of it.

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:33.686
-Yes.
-I think some people felt very

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.554
creatively stifled at that time.

00:02:35.554 --> 00:02:38.724
Did you very early on
in that time that we were all

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:40.760
spending alone think, "Oh, I'm
going to actually sit down

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.295
and try to write something?"

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:43.829
-I mean, pretty much, yeah.

00:02:43.829 --> 00:02:46.566
I mean, I went through the,
you know, watching

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:49.034
a bunch of movies,
and actually during that time,

00:02:49.034 --> 00:02:51.303
I was watching movies
that I grew up on.

00:02:51.303 --> 00:02:56.008
Like "Manhattan," "Annie Hall,"
"When Harry Met Sally."

00:02:56.008 --> 00:02:58.077
Like, movies that I knew
the ending,

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:01.079
it was comforting,
there was joy.

00:03:01.079 --> 00:03:04.116
And so I thought, well,
I've been -- I was reading a lot

00:03:04.116 --> 00:03:06.819
about, you know, couples coming
together, falling apart.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:10.022
It was like, "I think with the
threat of the world ending,

00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:11.490
let's see a couple
come together."

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.025
-Yeah, that's good.

00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:14.160
So funny you say that.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.861
I also feel like
in early pandemic

00:03:15.861 --> 00:03:17.997
I only wanted to engage
with things where I knew

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:20.099
the ending, and it was --
you know, we were all

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:21.901
living this time where none
of us knew what the ending was.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.069
-Exactly.
I was like, "I think

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.937
I'm gonna -- I am going to watch
"Moonstruck."

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:26.839
You know what I mean?
-Exactly, exactly.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:28.307
And I watched that too,
many times.

00:03:28.307 --> 00:03:30.075
-That a winner, right? Yeah.
-Ah.

00:03:30.075 --> 00:03:32.111
-What kind of director
do you find yourself to be?

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:33.980
And do you feel like you
borrowed from the directors

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.648
you've liked over your career?

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:36.816
-Definitely.

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:39.118
I'm definitely...

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:42.021
I love actors, and some of the
best experiences

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:46.959
I had came from working
with Curtis Hanson,

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:51.163
Christopher Nolan and Ang Lee.

00:03:51.163 --> 00:03:53.900
And then also doing
"All My Sons" on Broadway.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.237
Just that eight-week
rehearsal experience.

00:03:58.237 --> 00:04:01.306
I just felt so blessed
because these directors

00:04:01.306 --> 00:04:05.277
all just took the time to help,
you know, create a character.

00:04:05.277 --> 00:04:10.916
So I try to give my actors
and myself the space to create

00:04:10.916 --> 00:04:14.220
the characters
and really play and improvise.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.788
-You obviously
are an actor as well.

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:17.823
Are you hard to direct?

00:04:17.823 --> 00:04:20.827
-[ Laughs ] Always.

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:23.329
I mean, every day
I struggle with that.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:24.864
-Is it tricky, like,
when you're in a scene

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:27.766
with another actor and you're
also the director, obviously?

00:04:27.766 --> 00:04:29.568
I mean, you're not the first
person that's had to deal

00:04:29.568 --> 00:04:32.438
with this, but I feel like
the other actors in the scene

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:34.006
have to feel like
you're paying attention

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:35.441
to them the same way --

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.210
-And I am.
-Right.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.211
-But what I didn't know
until today,

00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:40.446
because I was doing interviews

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:42.147
with Jim Sturgess,
who plays Charlie.

00:04:42.147 --> 00:04:43.216
-Who was in the clip there,
yeah.

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:44.450
-Yes.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:45.584
He's like -- I forgot --

00:04:45.584 --> 00:04:48.087
but he's like,
"Katie never yells cut."

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:50.956
And I forgot that, because
I like to sort of,

00:04:50.956 --> 00:04:53.726
what else you gonna do?
You know?

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:55.160
And he was like, "Every day
I didn't know

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:56.229
what was going to happen."

00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:57.763
I was like, yeah,
but that's the fun of it.

00:04:57.763 --> 00:05:00.266
I mean, the script
is sort of just there.

00:05:00.266 --> 00:05:02.402
So I guess I'm unpredictable.

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:03.569
-That's good.
-Yeah.

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:04.903
-That's a good thing
for a director to be.

00:05:04.903 --> 00:05:06.973
You wrote yourself
as a food critic in this.

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:08.875
Do you think you would make
a good food critic?

00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:10.976
Because I don't think I would be
a good food critic.

00:05:10.976 --> 00:05:13.012
-Um, I would enjoy it.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:17.383
I mean, I love Ruth Reichl
and I mean, going in with a wig

00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:18.784
and that would be kind of fun.

00:05:18.784 --> 00:05:20.420
-Oh, this is -- right.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.087
I mean, that's a real --
-They can't -- they can't

00:05:22.087 --> 00:05:24.657
know you're there, so you get to
disguise yourself.

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:26.725
-But I feel like...

00:05:26.725 --> 00:05:29.295
I mean, what a great job.

00:05:29.295 --> 00:05:30.763
-Yeah.

00:05:30.763 --> 00:05:33.032
But I wouldn't -- I imagine
the best meal

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:34.467
you've ever had, right?

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:36.736
Would it be worse if you had
to wear a fake wig?

00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:38.303
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.303 --> 00:05:39.605
Like, that would be my thing.
-I know, right?

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:41.040
I'd always be worried
my fake moustache

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.575
would fall into the soup.
-Exactly.

00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:44.209
And do you have to pretend
it's not --

00:05:44.209 --> 00:05:46.445
Yeah, you have to,
that it's not the best meal.

00:05:46.445 --> 00:05:47.746
-Right.
-I'm not good at that.

00:05:47.746 --> 00:05:49.582
-That's the thing.
I feel like most of the time

00:05:49.582 --> 00:05:51.417
I'm just super happy
at a restaurant

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:53.252
like when the food gets there.

00:05:53.252 --> 00:05:54.387
I feel like
if I was a food critic,

00:05:54.387 --> 00:05:56.322
I'd always be like,
"Another a-plus.

00:05:56.322 --> 00:06:01.127
I ordered it, I ordered it,
it came, I ate it."

00:06:01.127 --> 00:06:04.196
-Yeah. Your -- your opinion
would probably --

00:06:04.196 --> 00:06:05.932
probably lose the value there.

00:06:05.932 --> 00:06:08.400
-How -- like, when you write,
who are the people that you

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.402
sort of show your work to over
the course of the process?

00:06:10.402 --> 00:06:11.870
-My poor friends.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:13.539
And I bug them
until they read it.

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:14.907
-Okay.
-Sometimes I make them read it

00:06:14.907 --> 00:06:17.176
over the phone with me.

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:20.413
But yeah, for this, I actually
reached out to my friend

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:23.616
Dito Montiel, who did "Guide to
Recognizing Your Saints."

00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:25.651
I love that movie.

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:29.088
And we worked together on
"The Son of No One."

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:32.491
And he helped me out a lot.

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:36.862
And then Derek Luke
and Jim Sturgess,

00:06:36.862 --> 00:06:38.797
we wrote a lot of it together.

00:06:38.797 --> 00:06:41.701
I mean, they signed on,
and then it was like, you know,

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:43.936
I do have some thoughts here
and some thoughts there.

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:46.438
I was like, "Great.
Let's do that."

00:06:46.438 --> 00:06:47.806
-That's very nice.
You're very collaborative.

00:06:47.806 --> 00:06:50.143
That's the right kind
of person to work with.

00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:51.510
-Yeah.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.646
I mean, I always think
that it's just, yeah,

00:06:53.646 --> 00:06:55.281
it's going to make it better.
I don't know.

00:06:55.281 --> 00:06:58.684
I mean, my ideas are fine,
but I'm sure --

00:06:58.684 --> 00:07:01.120
I know there's a lot better
ideas out there.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.389
-Do you like shooting
in New York City?

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:05.224
Because obviously
you've done that a lot.

00:07:05.224 --> 00:07:06.492
-I do.

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:08.126
-Do you feel like New Yorkers
are excited

00:07:08.126 --> 00:07:09.195
when they see something
shooting?

00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:11.731
Or they're already over it?

00:07:11.731 --> 00:07:13.766
-Most people
are already over it.

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:15.434
-Yeah.
-I always feel like in New York,

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:17.503
I think everybody
thinks they're a celebrity.

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:18.838
You know what I mean?
-Oh, yeah. Absolutely.

00:07:18.838 --> 00:07:21.407
-Everybody thinks where
they're going

00:07:21.407 --> 00:07:23.676
is more important
than where you're going.

00:07:23.676 --> 00:07:26.211
-It's so true.

00:07:26.211 --> 00:07:29.849
But I'm the exception,
because I'm always --

00:07:29.849 --> 00:07:31.951
I get excited
when I see a movie truck.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:33.653
-I do, too.
-I'm always like --

00:07:33.653 --> 00:07:36.422
I try to find the one
person that might tell me,

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:38.457
because they're not supposed to.
-Sure.

00:07:38.457 --> 00:07:40.893
-So usually they'll be like,
"It's a student film."

00:07:40.893 --> 00:07:42.361
-I'm like,
"I've used that excuse.

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:44.897
Come on.
What is it, what is it?"

00:07:44.897 --> 00:07:46.198
-Do you think they're like,
"Katie Holmes

00:07:46.198 --> 00:07:47.666
is sniffing around
this movie set?"

00:07:47.666 --> 00:07:49.735
-I don't know.
I don't know.

00:07:49.735 --> 00:07:51.436
Maybe I have a reputation.

00:07:51.436 --> 00:07:55.240
-I walk by and I'm like --
I'll see trailers

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:57.609
and they'll have like a star
on the door but not a name.

00:07:57.609 --> 00:08:00.145
And I want to walk up
and be like, hey, there's a high

00:08:00.145 --> 00:08:02.481
likelihood I know
who's in there.

00:08:02.481 --> 00:08:03.816
-Yeah.
-So why don't you go

00:08:03.816 --> 00:08:04.817
tell them I'm here.

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:06.018
-Yeah.

00:08:06.018 --> 00:08:08.921
Yeah, it's kind of --
it's kind of humiliating.

00:08:08.921 --> 00:08:10.890
-It's really humiliating.
-Now that you mention it.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.359
-Also there's times where
I'm walking by,

00:08:13.359 --> 00:08:16.294
and I feel like --
even though I'm --

00:08:16.294 --> 00:08:17.729
I feel like they somehow
think like

00:08:17.729 --> 00:08:20.332
I'm there creeping
on the film set.

00:08:20.332 --> 00:08:21.967
And I'm like,
what do they think?

00:08:21.967 --> 00:08:24.103
That I'm like out
looking for guests?

00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:25.805
Like --
[ Laughter ]

00:08:25.805 --> 00:08:28.373
That I'm like, "Oh, we don't
have anybody for Thursday.

00:08:28.373 --> 00:08:29.741
I'll go hang
around the trailers."

00:08:29.741 --> 00:08:31.010
-Yeah, exactly.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.779
You have the list of,
like, New York locations.

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:37.216
But for -- we did a movie this
fall called "Rare Objects"

00:08:37.216 --> 00:08:39.117
that I also directed,
and we shot

00:08:39.117 --> 00:08:41.988
like a block from where I live,
which was fantastic.

00:08:41.988 --> 00:08:46.391
And we also shot up in Queens,
and so we --

00:08:46.391 --> 00:08:49.228
it was a real love letter
to New York,

00:08:49.228 --> 00:08:51.697
and such a great experience,
to, you know,

00:08:51.697 --> 00:08:53.332
just shoot all over the city.

00:08:53.332 --> 00:08:58.771
And -- and so, yeah, it was --
it was quite fun.

00:08:58.771 --> 00:09:01.607
I mean, to go back
to your original, like, yeah,

00:09:01.607 --> 00:09:02.908
I love shooting in New York.
-It's nice.

00:09:02.908 --> 00:09:06.245
It is really nice to be able
to present New York City.

00:09:06.245 --> 00:09:08.246
I mean, again, those movies you
were talking about early on.

00:09:08.246 --> 00:09:10.615
You know, those are --
those -- a lot of those films

00:09:10.615 --> 00:09:11.851
are the reason why
I wanted to come

00:09:11.851 --> 00:09:12.985
to New York City
in the first place.

00:09:12.985 --> 00:09:14.253
-Me, too.
-It looks really good

00:09:14.253 --> 00:09:15.854
on a big screen.

00:09:15.854 --> 00:09:17.690
Anything you can do to make
people -- remind people

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:19.425
what a great place
it is to come to visit,

00:09:19.425 --> 00:09:20.959
especially at this weather.

00:09:20.959 --> 00:09:22.494
-Especially, come now.

00:09:22.494 --> 00:09:24.730
-You've got to come now.
-Yeah, come now. It's fabulous.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.632
-Thank you so much.
Congrats on the film.

00:09:26.632 --> 00:09:27.800
-Thank you.
-It's such a delight to see you.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:28.900
You guys, that's Katie Holmes.

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:30.903
"Alone Together"
in select theaters

00:09:30.903 --> 00:09:33.372
July 22nd and On Demand
July 29th.

00:09:33.372 --> 00:09:35.274
We'll be right back
with Joe Pera, everybody!

